I’ll begin with the public pattern and metadata, then inspect only the framework’s analysis/tooling guidance needed to run candidate normalization, physical screening, and GSAS-II. I’ll keep candidate identification evidence-based and avoid generator/scorer internals as requested.

The public chemistry is ZnO + WO₃ fired at 750 °C, so Zn–W–O phases and residual precursors are the first plausible inventory—not assumptions about how many phases are present. I’m now extracting peak families and locating the supplied candidate-library/physical-screen utilities before proposing any structure.

The peak positions already separate three coherent families: wolframite-like ZnWO₄, wurtzite ZnO, and monoclinic WO₃. That is only a working inventory; I’m preparing multiple independent parents for each family (including an alternate ZnWO₄ polymorph) and will retain phases only if whole-pattern residual/deletion tests support them.

The first automated refinement exposed a numerical issue from fitting weak, position-misaligned phases simultaneously. I’ve corrected the sequence to register the dominant wolframite phase first, enforce positive phase scales, and only then release all phase cells/scales. The corrected comparison branch is now running.

The simple zero-offset nuisance branch is currently slightly better and more stable (Rwp 11.61%) than the specimen-displacement restart (11.67% and still drifting). I’m using the stable branch only for the source/inventory screen, with fixed coordinates and identical flexibility; the displacement branch remains available as the required independent restart for the later final-model check.

The source/inventory screen is decisive: the best passing models are wolframite ZnWO₄ (COD-derived Zn/Cd parent 1520642), wurtzite ZnO, and monoclinic P2₁/c WO₃ (COD 2311041). Deleting ZnO, WO₃, or ZnWO₄ raises Rwp to 12.55%, 20.87%, and 36.99%, respectively. A first effect test shows physically interpretable ZnWO₄ broadening (about 0.10 µm isotropic size) and lowers Rwp to 10.40%; I’m checking competing strain/texture explanations before retaining it.

The stable low-complexity branch now fits at Rwp 5.04% (counting-statistics lower bound 2.69%). Retained effects are WO₃ [001] March–Dollase texture, isotropic ZnWO₄ microstrain, and refined polarization; size is rejected because strain alone fits better, and roughness/secondary textures are negligible. I’m at the final selective-coordinate stage and will keep only changes that reproduce and remain physically passing.

The nuisance restarts exposed one important instability: ZnWO₄ coordinates, cells, and fractions are stable, but refined WO₃ W positions co-drift across background branches and become >0.95 correlated. I’m reverting those WO₃ coordinates to the published parent, as required by the stability criterion, while retaining the reproducible ZnWO₄ coordinate improvement.

One more model hygiene issue surfaced in the project audit: GSAS-II’s nominal defaults (1 µm size and 1000 ppm strain) are active even when their refinement flags are off. I’m removing those untested fixed contributions, re-establishing the common profile, and then re-testing phase-specific broadening from true zero. This is necessary before the final export can be considered physical and defensible.

I’m at the final model-selection check: the three-phase assignment is secure, and I’m testing whether the modest ZnWO₄ size-broadening improvement remains stable after a clean restart. I’ll keep it only if it survives without bound hits or extreme correlations, then regenerate and independently replay every deliverable.

The first clean restart improved the fit but also moved the ZnWO₄ size/strain pair appreciably, so it was not yet converged. I’m continuing that same branch to convergence before judging it; the key acceptance tests are repeatability, no frozen/bound parameters, and a correlation below the project’s “extreme” threshold.

A simultaneous reset exposed the expected size/profile coupling and became singular, so I’m rejecting that restart itself—not treating its worse fit as evidence. I’m rerunning the same alternative in controlled stages (background/profile first, then cells/coordinates, then strain and size) so the restart is a fair stability test. The independent specimen-shift branch has converged but remains worse than the zero-offset branch.

The controlled restart passed cleanly: no frozen parameters, final Rwp 3.665% with 10 background terms, versus 3.671% with 8; fractions differed by at most 0.04 wt%. That improvement is negligible, so I’m selecting the simpler 8-term model. ZnWO₄ size and strain also reproduced closely (0.253 vs 0.257 µm; 2874 vs 2951 ppm), supporting their retention.

The selected project has now been exported and replayed without re-optimization over all 8,301 measured points. Replay gives Rwp 3.669%, Rp 2.803%, GOF 1.366, and exactly reproduces the project fractions. I’m now running the independent CIF geometry, export-consistency, and fit-grid checks before writing the final report.

Completed the three-phase refinement:

- ZnWO₄ (wolframite): 70.1415 wt%
- ZnO (wurtzite): 8.5459 wt%
- WO₃ (monoclinic): 21.3127 wt%

Independent full-grid replay: Rwp 3.6693%, Rp 2.8026%, GOF 1.3659. Fractions sum to 1.0, all CIFs pass the physical screen, and the replayed project agrees with the submitted structures and profile.

